#!/usr/bin/python3
"""Send pages, files and text between a phone and the FlickOS computer.

The phone runs the KDE Connect app (F-Droid, Google Play), unchanged; this
program speaks the part of the KDE Connect protocol (version 8) that moving
things needs: pairing, kdeconnect.share.request (a page, text, a file),
kdeconnect.clipboard and kdeconnect.ping, plus the phone's battery (for the
panel button's tooltip) and kdeconnect.findmyphone.request (ring the phone).
Nothing else is announced, so the phone's app hides its other features
(remote input, commands, ...).

The panel button (only while on) opens `flickos-phone menu`: send files,
typed text, the clipboard or a screenshot, ring the phone, what came from it, Settings.

Off by default (flickos-phone set enabled yes). While off nothing runs. While
on, `flickos-phone daemon` (started by FlickOS's autostart, never in the live
session) runs for the session: Python's standard library, no GTK (only
`send --choose`, the menu's Send Files to Phone..., opens GTK's file
chooser, in its own short-lived process). It listens
only while the computer is on a network where a phone was paired (the
NetworkManager connections in `networks`), or while `flickos-phone pair` runs.

How a phone connects (docs/design/flickos-phone.md, ADR-001 and ADR-003):
the daemon broadcasts its identity to UDP 1716 and the phone connects to TCP
1716. In KDE Connect the device that opens the TCP connection is the TLS
*server*, so the daemon is always the TLS client: it never connects to a
phone's main port, and answers a phone's broadcast with its own identity sent
straight back, so the phone connects. That is what lets Python's ssl module
check an unknown phone's certificate (for pairing) without a verify callback.
A paired phone's certificate is pinned (compared byte for byte).

Files: identity and paired phones in $XDG_DATA_HOME/flickos/phone/, what came
from a phone in $XDG_STATE_HOME/flickos/phone/received.jsonl, settings in
/etc/xdg/flickos/phone.conf then ~/.config/flickos/phone ([Phone]), the
daemon's lock and control socket in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/flickos/phone/. Files a
phone sends go into the Downloads folder. Every change of a setting is
recorded for Settings > History.

Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 error, 2 usage error.
"""

